# An exposed Thing with explicit ingress policy and an exact handler boundary

## Goal

Expose a simulated Thing through `Wotex.Runtime.ExposedThing` behind the
Lab's reference host and see exactly which requests reach a handler: only a
request whose Form declares the operation, whose credential satisfies the
Form's security and whose input fits the DataSchema is dispatched. Every
refusal leaves the handler call counter unchanged. The fixture's
expected-output file lists the vectors this notebook replays.

## Prerequisites

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:wotex_lab, "~> 0.1.0"}
])
```

The requirement above is a published-artifact requirement. At the time this
notebook was written none of the `wotex*` packages is published on Hex; the
notebook is verified against the workspace source cohort recorded in
`priv/provenance/source-cohort.json` by `test/wotex/lab/cookbook_test.exs`,
which evaluates every Elixir cell below in order (skipping this install
cell). `Mix.install/1` never points at a Git checkout. No network is used:
the loopback transport carries runtime requests to a process in this VM.

## Package and consumer ownership

| Concern | Owner | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Exposed Thing handler table and dispatch | `wotex_runtime` (package-implements) | `Wotex.Runtime.ExposedThing.new/2`, `dispatch/5` |
| Consumed side: selection, identity, results | `wotex_runtime` | `Wotex.Runtime.ConsumedThing` |
| Ingress policy: declared operations, security, input bounds, subscriptions | `wotex_lab` host (consumer) | `Wotex.Lab.Reference.Thing` |
| Loopback transport and credential ports | `wotex_lab` (consumer-implements) | `Adapters.Runtime.Loopback`, `NoSec`, `StaticRef` |
| Supervision | consumer (`wotex_lab` instance) | `Wotex.Lab.start_child/3` |
| The simulated effect of `setTarget` | this notebook | the `:actions` option below |

## Run

```elixir
alias Wotex.Lab
alias Wotex.Lab.Adapters.Runtime.{Loopback, StaticRef}
alias Wotex.Lab.Reference.Thing
alias Wotex.Lab.Telemetry
alias Wotex.Runtime.{BindingProfile, ConsumedThing, Context, Error, ExposedThing, FormSelector, Request, Result}
alias Wotex.ThingDescription

telemetry_id = "cookbook-expose-thing-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"

events =
  for component <- Telemetry.components(),
      operation <- Telemetry.operations(),
      event <- [:start, :stop, :exception],
      do: [:wotex, :lab, component, operation, event]

:ok = :telemetry.attach_many(telemetry_id, events, &Telemetry.forward/4, self())

fixture_dir = Application.app_dir(:wotex_lab, "priv/fixtures/loopback")
{:ok, td} = fixture_dir |> Path.join("thing-description.json") |> File.read!() |> ThingDescription.parse()
{:ok, expected} = fixture_dir |> Path.join("expected-output.json") |> File.read!() |> Wotex.JSON.decode()
vectors = expected["vectors"]
Map.keys(vectors)
```

Start an instance and place the host under its `:things` role. The host
takes the TD, the initial state, the expected token for `bearer_sc`, and an
explicit effect for `setTarget`: the only way an Action changes state here.

```elixir
token = "room-token-7f3a"
{:ok, lab} = Lab.start_link(id: "expose-thing", max_children: 8)

{:ok, host} =
  Lab.start_child(
    lab,
    :things,
    {Thing,
     td: td,
     state: %{"temperature" => 20.0, "target" => 21.0},
     tokens: %{"bearer_sc" => token},
     actions: %{
       "setTarget" => fn input, state ->
         {:ok, %{"accepted" => true}, :accepted, Map.put(state, "target", input)}
       end
     }}
  )

Thing.stats(host)
```

The consumer side: a loopback binding profile, the loopback transport bound
to the host pid, and a credential port that resolves the bearer reference.

```elixir
{:ok, profile} =
  BindingProfile.new(id: :loopback, schemes: ["loopback"], operations: Wotex.Runtime.operations())

consume = fn lookup ->
  {:ok, consumed} =
    ConsumedThing.new(td,
      profiles: [profile],
      transports: %{loopback: {Loopback, %{host: host}}},
      credentials: {StaticRef, %{references: %{"bearer_sc" => "vault://room/token"}, lookup: lookup}}
    )

  consumed
end

consumed = consume.(fn "vault://room/token" -> {:ok, token} end)
context = Context.new!(request_id: "expose", deadline: System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) + 1_000)
```

Positive vectors: read, write, invoke. Three handler calls, no rejection,
and the Action's explicit effect is visible in the host state.

```elixir
{:ok, %Result{payload: read_payload}} = ConsumedThing.read_property(consumed, "temperature", context)
{:ok, %Result{status: write_status}} = ConsumedThing.write_property(consumed, "target", 24.5, context)
{:ok, %Result{status: invoke_status}} = ConsumedThing.invoke_action(consumed, "setTarget", 23.0, context)
after_positive = Thing.stats(host)
{read_payload, write_status, invoke_status, after_positive}
```

## Deliberate breakage

Each negative vector is refused before a handler runs. The read-only Property
has no Form declaring `writeproperty`, so the runtime refuses it before the
transport; the others are refused by the host's admission and counted as
rejections.

```elixir
{:error, %Error{code: readonly_code}} =
  ConsumedThing.write_property(consumed, "temperature", 1.0, context)

{:error, %Error{class: range_class} = range} =
  ConsumedThing.write_property(consumed, "target", 99.0, context)

{:error, %Error{} = wrong_type} = ConsumedThing.write_property(consumed, "target", "hot", context)

stranger = consume.(fn _reference -> {:ok, "wrong"} end)
{:error, %Error{} = unauthorized} = ConsumedThing.write_property(stranger, "target", 22.0, context)

{:ok, selection} = FormSelector.select(td, :property, "temperature", :readproperty, [profile])
request = Request.from_selection(selection, context, nil)
thing_level = %{request | affordance_type: :thing, affordance_name: nil, operation: :readallproperties}
{:error, %Wotex.Lab.Error{code: thing_level_code}} = Thing.request(host, thing_level, nil)

after_negative = Thing.stats(host)

%{
  readonly: readonly_code,
  range: {range_class, range.details.cause.code},
  wrong_type: wrong_type.details.cause.code,
  unauthorized: unauthorized.details.cause.code,
  thing_level: thing_level_code,
  stats: after_negative
}
```

The package boundary itself, without the host: `ExposedThing.new/2` takes a
handler table keyed by operation and affordance name, and `dispatch/5` runs
exactly the handler that was declared, or returns `:handler_not_found`.

```elixir
handlers = %{
  {:readproperty, "temperature"} => fn _input, _context -> {:ok, 19.5, :ok, :unchanged} end
}

{:ok, exposed} = ExposedThing.new(td, handlers)
direct_context = Context.new!(request_id: "direct")
direct_read = ExposedThing.dispatch(exposed, :readproperty, "temperature", nil, direct_context)
direct_missing = ExposedThing.dispatch(exposed, :writeproperty, "target", 22.0, direct_context)
{direct_read, direct_missing}
```

```elixir
:ok = Supervisor.stop(lab)
```

## Safe telemetry

Runtime request spans name the operation, the `:loopback` profile and the
outcome. The Thing id, the token and the payloads are absent.

```elixir
drain = fn drain ->
  receive do
    {:wotex_lab_telemetry, event, measurements, metadata} ->
      [{event, measurements, metadata} | drain.(drain)]
  after
    0 -> []
  end
end

spans = drain.(drain)
:telemetry.detach(telemetry_id)

outcomes =
  for {[:wotex, :lab, :runtime, :request, :stop], _measurements, metadata} <- spans,
      do: {metadata.operation, metadata.outcome}

text = inspect(spans)
labels_safe? = not (text =~ token) and not (text =~ "urn:wotex") and not (text =~ "24.5")
{outcomes, labels_safe?}
```

## Expected output

| Check | Expectation |
| --- | --- |
| `handler-boundary-counts` | Three handler calls after the positive vectors; the four host-refused negatives add rejections and no calls |
| `operation-not-declared` | Writing the read-only Property is `:compatible_form_not_found` before any transport |
| `out-of-range-refused` | `99.0` is `:permanent` with cause `:input_out_of_range`; `"hot"` is `:invalid_input_type` |
| `unauthorized-refused` | A wrong token is `:unauthorized` and the token never appears |
| `thing-level-unsupported` | Thing-level operations are `:unsupported_operation` |
| `explicit-effect` | `setTarget` changed `target` to `23.0` only through the declared effect |
| `exposed-thing-direct` | The direct handler answers `19.5`; an undeclared operation is `:handler_not_found` |

```elixir
%{
  "handler-boundary-counts" =>
    after_positive.handler_calls == 3 and after_positive.rejected == 0 and
      after_negative.handler_calls == 3 and after_negative.rejected == 4,
  "operation-not-declared" => readonly_code == :compatible_form_not_found,
  "out-of-range-refused" =>
    range_class == :permanent and range.details.cause.code == :input_out_of_range and
      wrong_type.details.cause.code == :invalid_input_type,
  "unauthorized-refused" =>
    unauthorized.details.cause.code == :unauthorized and not (inspect(unauthorized) =~ token) and
      labels_safe?,
  "thing-level-unsupported" => thing_level_code == :unsupported_operation,
  "explicit-effect" =>
    read_payload == vectors["loopback:read-temperature"]["payload"] and
      after_negative.state["target"] == 23.0 and write_status == :ok and invoke_status == :accepted,
  "exposed-thing-direct" =>
    direct_read == {:ok, 19.5, :ok, :unchanged} and
      match?({:error, %Error{code: :handler_not_found}}, direct_missing)
}
```

## Public seam

`runtime.transport` — `Wotex.Runtime.Transport.request/3`, `subscribe/4`,
`unsubscribe/4`, `decode_frame/3` (consumer-implements; `Loopback`).
`runtime.credentials` — `Wotex.Runtime.Credentials.resolve/4` (`StaticRef`).

## Spec and completion IDs

* Lab: WLB.04 (`WLB-C04`, `WLB-C08`).
* Upstream consumer evidence: `RT-C03`.

## Replacement adapter

To expose a Thing over a real protocol, keep `ExposedThing.new/2` as the
handler boundary and put your own ingress in front of it: decode the wire
request, check that the selected Form declares the operation, check the
Form's security against the credential you received, validate the input, and
only then call `dispatch/5`. The Lab host is the reference for that order;
nothing in the runtime package performs those checks for you.
